1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,290
In May 1988, fighter jets fired missiles

2
00:00:03,340 --> 00:00:06,430
at the biggest ship
humans have ever built.

3
00:00:06,640 --> 00:00:08,550
The ship burst into flames,

4
00:00:08,700 --> 00:00:11,490
and its owners wrote
it off as a total loss.

5
00:00:12,080 --> 00:00:15,230
This ship was four hundred
and fifty-eight metres long.

6
00:00:15,380 --> 00:00:18,810
Stand it on its end, and it's
taller than the Petronas Towers.

7
00:00:19,220 --> 00:00:21,910
It was so big it couldn't
fit through the Suez Canal,

8
00:00:22,220 --> 00:00:26,250
the Panama Canal, or even the English
Channel when it was fully loaded.

9
00:00:26,780 --> 00:00:29,490
If the captain went full
astern at top speed,

10
00:00:29,740 --> 00:00:32,110
it needed about nine kilometres to stop.

11
00:00:32,180 --> 00:00:34,190
And that fire wasn't the end.

12
00:00:34,340 --> 00:00:38,130
This ship was rebuilt, and sailed
again under four new names.

13
00:00:38,340 --> 00:00:40,150
So how do you build something this big...

14
00:00:40,360 --> 00:00:42,850
and why did the world decide
never to build one again?

15
00:00:42,900 --> 00:00:44,650
It's 1974, in Japan.

16
00:00:44,960 --> 00:00:46,790
At the Oppama shipyard near Tokyo,

17
00:00:47,020 --> 00:00:49,550
Sumitomo Heavy Industries
is building a monster.

18
00:00:49,760 --> 00:00:51,570
It doesn't even have a name yet.

19
00:00:51,820 --> 00:00:53,470
Just a number: hull 1016.

20
00:00:53,520 --> 00:00:56,430
It's a supertanker made
for one job: carrying

21
00:00:56,480 --> 00:00:58,870
as much crude oil as possible in one trip.

22
00:00:59,020 --> 00:01:01,630
Bigger ship, more oil,
cheaper oil per barrel.

23
00:01:01,900 --> 00:01:02,810
That was the idea.

24
00:01:02,860 --> 00:01:05,210
But during sea trials,
something goes wrong.

25
00:01:05,580 --> 00:01:09,150
When the ship goes astern, in
reverse, it shakes violently.

26
00:01:09,820 --> 00:01:12,890
The Greek shipowner who
ordered it refuses to take it.

27
00:01:13,080 --> 00:01:14,080
Lawyers get involved.

28
00:01:14,380 --> 00:01:17,410
And for years, one of the largest
objects ever built just...

29
00:01:17,460 --> 00:01:18,710
sits there.

30
00:01:19,080 --> 00:01:22,610
Eventually, the shipyard sells
it under a new name: Oppama.

31
00:01:22,660 --> 00:01:24,610
And that's when a shipping tycoon from

32
00:01:24,660 --> 00:01:26,570
Hong Kong walks in with a wild idea.

33
00:01:27,040 --> 00:01:31,010
His name is C. Y. Tung, the
founder of the OOCL shipping line.

34
00:01:31,180 --> 00:01:32,870
He looks at one of the
biggest ships in the world,

35
00:01:32,920 --> 00:01:34,850
and he says: make it bigger.

36
00:01:35,320 --> 00:01:37,910
So the shipyard does something
that sounds impossible.

37
00:01:38,360 --> 00:01:39,530
They lengthen the ship.

38
00:01:39,700 --> 00:01:42,850
They cut into the hull and build
in a brand new section of steel.

39
00:01:43,300 --> 00:01:45,620
That one change adds about a hundred and

40
00:01:45,670 --> 00:01:47,910
forty-six thousand
tonnes of cargo capacity.

41
00:01:48,180 --> 00:01:50,490
That's more than many normal
tankers can carry in total.

42
00:01:50,540 --> 00:01:55,670
In 1979, it returns to the water
with a new name: Seawise Giant.

43
00:01:56,140 --> 00:01:58,610
Let's put this in numbers
you can actually picture.

44
00:02:00,480 --> 00:02:02,130
Length: four hundred
and fifty-eight metres.

45
00:02:02,540 --> 00:02:06,270
That's longer than four and a half
football fields laid end to end.

46
00:02:06,600 --> 00:02:08,710
Width: about sixty-nine metres.

47
00:02:09,120 --> 00:02:11,750
You could park a twenty-lane
highway across its deck.

48
00:02:11,980 --> 00:02:14,010
Under the water, when fully loaded,

49
00:02:14,180 --> 00:02:16,710
the hull went down almost
twenty-five metres.

50
00:02:17,040 --> 00:02:19,850
That's like sinking an eight-storey
building below the waves.

51
00:02:19,900 --> 00:02:23,590
It could carry about four point
one million barrels of oil.

52
00:02:23,900 --> 00:02:26,850
Its deck covered more than
thirty-one thousand square metres,

53
00:02:27,140 --> 00:02:29,150
around four football pitches of steel.

54
00:02:29,480 --> 00:02:32,270
Its rudder alone weighed two
hundred and thirty tonnes.

55
00:02:32,840 --> 00:02:34,410
The propeller weighed fifty tonnes.

56
00:02:34,620 --> 00:02:37,030
And when it was fully loaded,
the whole ship weighed

57
00:02:37,080 --> 00:02:39,810
more than six hundred and
fifty thousand tonnes.

58
00:02:40,480 --> 00:02:42,990
A steam turbine of fifty
thousand horsepower pushed

59
00:02:43,040 --> 00:02:44,990
all of that through the
sea at about sixteen

60
00:02:45,040 --> 00:02:47,850
and a half knots, around
thirty kilometres an hour.

61
00:02:47,900 --> 00:02:49,190
That sounds slow.

62
00:02:49,240 --> 00:02:51,010
Until you try to stop.

63
00:02:51,360 --> 00:02:53,870
One of its captains said
that from full speed,

64
00:02:54,220 --> 00:02:56,810
it needed about nine
kilometres to come to a stop.

65
00:02:57,000 --> 00:03:00,890
To turn around, it needed a circle
about three kilometres wide.

66
00:03:01,180 --> 00:03:03,330
And it couldn't go where most ships go.

67
00:03:03,640 --> 00:03:05,230
Too wide for the Panama Canal.

68
00:03:05,640 --> 00:03:07,230
Too deep for the Suez Canal.

69
00:03:07,540 --> 00:03:10,570
Fully loaded, even the English
Channel was too shallow.

70
00:03:10,640 --> 00:03:12,770
So the biggest ship in the
world was locked out of

71
00:03:12,820 --> 00:03:15,370
some of the most important
shipping routes in the world.

72
00:03:15,460 --> 00:03:18,610
But in the 1980s, there
was one place where a giant

73
00:03:18,660 --> 00:03:21,270
floating oil tank was
exactly what someone needed.

74
00:03:21,520 --> 00:03:23,170
And that place was a war zone.

75
00:03:23,380 --> 00:03:25,310
Iran and Iraq are at war.

76
00:03:25,740 --> 00:03:29,050
Both sides start attacking oil
tankers in the Persian Gulf,

77
00:03:29,100 --> 00:03:30,990
trying to choke off each other's money.

78
00:03:31,280 --> 00:03:33,150
It becomes known as the Tanker War.

79
00:03:33,360 --> 00:03:37,330
Seawise Giant is used to store
and move Iranian crude oil.

80
00:03:37,500 --> 00:03:39,350
On the fourteenth of May, 1988,

81
00:03:39,620 --> 00:03:42,490
it's anchored off Larak Island,
near the Strait of Hormuz.

82
00:03:42,540 --> 00:03:43,990
Iraqi aircraft arrive.

83
00:03:44,360 --> 00:03:45,970
They fire French-made Exocet missiles.

84
00:03:46,020 --> 00:03:47,470
The missiles hit.

85
00:03:47,720 --> 00:03:49,690
Fire spreads across the oil on the water.

86
00:03:49,960 --> 00:03:52,710
The biggest ship in the world
becomes a burning wreck.

87
00:03:52,800 --> 00:03:54,870
The insurers declare it a total loss.

88
00:03:55,260 --> 00:03:58,630
For almost any other ship, that
would be the end of the story.

89
00:03:58,980 --> 00:04:01,590
After the war ends, a Norwegian company

90
00:04:01,640 --> 00:04:04,470
looks at the burned
giant and buys it anyway.

91
00:04:04,920 --> 00:04:08,730
It's towed to Singapore, where
the Keppel shipyard repairs it.

92
00:04:08,820 --> 00:04:13,550
In October 1991, it goes back to
work with a new name: Happy Giant.

93
00:04:13,600 --> 00:04:16,990
The same year, Norwegian
shipowner Jørgen Jahre buys it

94
00:04:17,040 --> 00:04:20,570
for thirty-nine million dollars
and renames it Jahre Viking.

95
00:04:20,980 --> 00:04:24,490
For more than ten years, it
carries oil around the world again.

96
00:04:24,640 --> 00:04:28,630
In 2004, it's sold once more
and renamed Knock Nevis.

97
00:04:28,940 --> 00:04:30,290
This time it doesn't sail.

98
00:04:30,500 --> 00:04:34,210
It's anchored off Qatar and used
as a floating oil storage tank.

99
00:04:34,720 --> 00:04:37,530
So if one giant ship
can carry so much oil,

100
00:04:37,760 --> 00:04:39,890
why don't we build them like this anymore?

101
00:04:40,260 --> 00:04:42,310
In the late sixties and early seventies,

102
00:04:42,360 --> 00:04:46,010
shipping companies ordered dozens
of these ultra-large tankers.

103
00:04:46,160 --> 00:04:48,550
Then the 1973 oil crisis hit,

104
00:04:48,600 --> 00:04:51,130
and the oil trade they
were built for changed.

105
00:04:51,580 --> 00:04:54,210
Big ships only make money
when they're full and moving.

106
00:04:54,500 --> 00:04:57,470
A ship this size could
only use a few ports,

107
00:04:57,580 --> 00:05:01,010
couldn't take shortcuts, and
took a long time to fill.

108
00:05:01,480 --> 00:05:04,110
France built four giants
of the Batillus class,

109
00:05:04,300 --> 00:05:07,610
each over five hundred and fifty
thousand tonnes deadweight.

110
00:05:07,840 --> 00:05:10,830
Three of them were scrapped after
less than ten years carrying oil.

111
00:05:10,880 --> 00:05:13,490
Today's big oil tankers are smaller.

112
00:05:13,840 --> 00:05:16,170
They give up some size
to get more flexibility.

113
00:05:16,780 --> 00:05:18,810
It turned out that being able to go almost

114
00:05:18,860 --> 00:05:21,690
anywhere is worth more
than being the biggest.

115
00:05:22,040 --> 00:05:25,330
In 2009, the old giant
makes one last trip,

116
00:05:25,380 --> 00:05:28,090
under its final name: Mont.

117
00:05:29,000 --> 00:05:30,710
On the twenty-second of December,

118
00:05:31,140 --> 00:05:33,690
it's driven onto the
beach at Alang, in India.

119
00:05:34,000 --> 00:05:37,350
A year later, it's gone,
cut apart piece by piece.

120
00:05:37,540 --> 00:05:38,530
But one piece survived.

121
00:05:38,580 --> 00:05:42,430
Its thirty-six-tonne anchor is
on display in Hong Kong today.

122
00:05:42,880 --> 00:05:47,650
In 2017, a floating gas plant called
Prelude finally beat its length.

123
00:05:47,880 --> 00:05:49,770
But Prelude is fixed to the seabed.

124
00:05:49,940 --> 00:05:53,230
It was built to sit in one
place, not to sail the world.

125
00:05:53,400 --> 00:05:56,770
So it was too big for the
canals, too big for most ports,

126
00:05:56,840 --> 00:05:59,950
and the world decided it
was too big to build again.

127
00:06:00,120 --> 00:06:01,730
And it was still the longest ship

128
00:06:01,780 --> 00:06:03,990
that ever crossed the world's oceans.

129
00:06:05,480 --> 00:06:08,930
If you want to see what happens
when a ship this size loses

130
00:06:08,980 --> 00:06:13,530
its engine in the middle of the
ocean, that video is right here.
